ith axes `t-SNE 1` and `t-SNE 2`. Points are labeled as `Reported`, `Predicted`, and `Others`. Gene labels include `EGFR` and `MYO1B`.\n\n- `c`: Bar chart for `NSCLC: A549` showing `Cell viability (versus NC)` after perturbing multiple genes. X-axis includes genes such as `MYO1B`, `NAV3`, `FLCN`, `WDR33`, `EIF2AK4`, `PIK3R4`, `BRCC3`, `ASXL1`, `EPO`, `GTF2H`, `HIPK2`, `GFPT1`, `MATR3`, `UCHL5`, `MARK3`, `NUP155`, `NR2C1`, `PGAM5`, `MAP2K5`, and `PPM1A`. Significance annotations are shown above selected bars.\n\n- `d`: Interface-like question-answer panel. A user asks whether `MYO1B` is involved in lung cancer functionally. The response attributed to `XunZi` states that `MYO1B` may influence lung cancer progression through cytoskeletal dynamics, cell migration, invasion, and pathways including PI3K/AKT and MAPK/ERK. It lists impacted genes including `AKT1`, `ERK`, `PIK3CA`, and `PTEN`.\n\n- `e`: Western blot panel comparing `Ctrl`, `siMYO1B-1`, and `siMYO1B-2`. Protein rows include `p-Erk`, `Erk`, `p-AKT`, `AKT`, `MYO1B`, and `Tubulin`, with molecular weights shown at left.\n\n- `f`: Quantification bar chart of `MYO1B/tubulin levels`, comparing `Ctrl`, `siMYO1B-1`, and `siMYO1B-2`.\n\n- `g`: Quantification bar chart of `p-Erk/Erk levels`, comparing `Ctrl`, `siMYO1B-1`, and `siMYO1B-2`.\n\n- `h`: Quantification bar chart of `p-AKT/AKT levels`, comparing `Ctrl`, `siMYO1B-1`, and `siMYO1B-2`.\n\n- `i`: Mouse experimental schematic. Day 1 treatment with `20 mg kg^-1 MPTP or saline, i.p., 4x`, followed by behavioral studies on `Days 19-22`.\n\n- `j`: Mouse experimental schematic. Day 1 `PFFs or PBS stereotaxic injection`, followed by behavioral studies on `Days 91-94`.\n\n- `k`: ROC curves for `PD-associated genes`, plotting `Sn` versus `1-Sp`. Methods include `XunZi`, `XunZi-R`, `XunZi-M`, `GPT-4o`, `DNN`, and `SVM`; `XunZi` has the highest visible AUC, `0.88`.\n\n- `l`: UMAP scatter plot with axes `UMAP 1` and `UMAP 2`, showing `Reported`, `Predicted`, and `Others`. A marker legend indicates `Kinase`. Labels include `LRRK2` and `PINK1`.\n\n- `m`: ROC curves for `PD-associated kinases`, again comparing `XunZi`, `XunZi-R`, `XunZi-M`, `GPT-4o`, `DNN`, and `SVM`. `XunZi` has the highest visible AUC, `0.92`.\n\n- `n`: t-SNE scatter plot with `Reported`, `Predicted`, and `Others` points. Labeled Parkinson’s disease-related kinase genes include `CHEK2`, `PINK1`, `STK13`, `IRAK4`, `DAPK2`, `LRRK2`, and `GRK5`.\n\n- `o`: Bar chart for `N2a` cells showing `Cell viability` across controls and genes including `mDpp+`, `Lrrk2`, `Cdk5`, `Pbk`, `Grk1`, `Chk2`, `Map2k2`, `Raf1`, `Trib3`, `Trib1`, `Irak4`, `Dapk2`, `Grk5`, `Ptk`, `Dyrk3`, `Map3k7`, `Stk33`, `Tssk6`, `Cdk2`, `Tnik`, `Stk32c`, and `Chk1`, with significance annotations.\n\nBiological source objects visible: NSCLC/A549 lung cancer cells, N2a neuronal cells, mouse Parkinson’s disease model schematics, genes/proteins/kinases including `MYO1B`, `EGFR`, `LRRK2`, `PINK1`, AKT/ERK signaling proteins, and PD-associated kinases.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces shown: ROC evaluation, t-SNE and UMAP embedding projections, predicted versus reported gene/kinase annotations, siRNA knockdown validation, western blot quantification, cell viability assays, and an LLM-like biomedical question-answer interface for `XunZi`.\n\nMain visible finding: `XunZi` outperforms comparator models in ROC analyses for NSCLC genes, PD-associated genes, and PD-associated kinases, and predicted candidates such as `MYO1B` and PD-related kinases are followed by experimental validation panels showing altered signaling or cell viability."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 6, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical figure labeled **a–m** about **CHEK2/Chk2 in Parkinson’s disease models**.\n\nPanel details:\n- **a**: Schematic comparison of model responses. A user asks whether **CHEK2** is functionally involved in Parkinson’s disease. **GPT-4o** answers “No,” while **XunZi** answers “Yes,” describing CHEK2 as linked to genome stability, DNA repair, oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, and listing impacted genes/pathways.\n- **b**: Western blot images from **SN** tissue comparing **saline** vs **MPTP** groups. Proteins shown: **Stk33, p-Irak4, Irak4, p-Chk2, Chk2, Tubulin**.\n- **c**: Bar plot quantifying **p-Chk2/Chk2 levels**, showing increased phosphorylation in **MPTP** vs saline.\n- **d**: Western blot images from **SN** comparing **PBS** vs **α-Syn PFFs** groups, with the same protein markers as panel b.\n- **e**: Bar plot quantifying **p-Chk2/Chk2 levels**, increased in **α-Syn PFFs** vs PBS.\n- **f**: Mouse experimental timeline: stereotaxic **AAV injection** on day 1, **MPTP or saline intraperitoneal injections** around day 28, behavioral studies on days 46–48, and immunoblotting/IHC on day 49.\n- **g**: Pole test bar plot. Groups include **AAV-control + saline**, **AAV-control + MPTP**, and **AAV-Chk2 KD + MPTP**. MPTP increases time to turn; Chk2 knockdown reduces impairment.\n- **h**: Rotarod test bar plot. MPTP reduces latency to fall; **AAV-Chk2 KD** improves performance relative to MPTP control.\n- **i**: Western blot from **SN** across AAV-control saline, AAV-control MPTP, and **AAV-Chk2 KD MPTP** groups. Proteins shown: **p-Chk2, Chk2, TH, Tubulin**.\n- **j**: Quantification of **p-Chk2/Chk2 levels**, increased by MPTP and reduced by **Chk2 knockdown**.\n- **k**: Quantification of **TH/tubulin levels**, decreased by MPTP and partially restored by **Chk2 knockdown**.\n- **l**: Immunohistochemistry images of dopaminergic neuron marker staining, comparing **AAV-vector + saline**, **AAV-vector + MPTP**, and **AAV-Chk2 KD + MPTP**.\n- **m**: Bar plot of **number of TH-positive neurons**, reduced in MPTP and increased with **AAV-Chk2 KD**.\n\nVisible biological source objects and assays:\n- Mouse Parkinson’s disease models using **MPTP** and **α-Syn PFFs**.\n- Brain region labeled **SN**, likely substantia nigra.\n- Molecular assays: western blotting, immunoblotting, immunohistochemistry.\n- Behavioral assays: pole test and rotarod test.\n- Viral manipulation: **AAV-mediated Chk2 knockdown**.\n\nVisible finding:\n- **Chk2 phosphorylation increases** in Parkinson’s disease models.\n- **AAV-Chk2 knockdown** reduces Chk2 activation, improves motor behavior, restores **TH** levels, and preserves **TH-positive neurons** relative to MPTP controls."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 8, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biomedical research figure labeled **a-o**.\n\nVisible content:\n- **a, b:** Bar/scatter plots for behavioral assays in mouse treatment groups. Panel a is **Pole test**, y-axis “Time to turn (s)”. Panel b is **Rotarod test**, y-axis “Latency to fall (s)”. Groups include **Sham**, **Sham + 2 µg CCT**, **MPTP**, **MPTP + DNL-201**, **MPTP + 0.2 µg CCT**, and **MPTP + 2 µg CCT**. Statistical brackets with p-values are shown.\n- **c:** Western blot panel from **SN** tissue, with lanes for Sham, MPTP, MPTP + 0.2 µg CCT, and MPTP + 2 µg CCT. Blots include **p-Lrrk2, Lrrk2, p-p53, p53, TH, p-Chk2, Chk2, p-Chk1, Chk1, Tubulin**.\n- **d, e:** Quantification bar plots for **p-Chk2/Chk2 levels** and **p-Lrrk2/Lrrk2 levels** across Sham, MPTP, and CCT-treated MPTP groups.\n- **f:** Immunohistochemistry microscopy images of dopaminergic neuron staining in SN-like tissue regions. Groups include Sham, Sham + 2 µg CCT, MPTP, MPTP + DNL-201, MPTP + 0.2 µg CCT, and MPTP + 2 µg CCT. Insets/boxed regions and scale bars are visible.\n- **g:** Bar plot quantifying **No. of TH-positive neurons** for the same MPTP/CCT/DNL-201 groups.\n- **h, i:** Behavioral assay bar/scatter plots for an **α-syn PFFs** model. Panel h is **Pole test**, panel i is **Rotarod test**. Groups include **Sham**, **α-syn PFFs**, and **α-syn PFFs + 2 µg CCT**.\n- **j:** Western blot panel from **SN** for α-syn PFFs model, with blots labeled **p-p53, p53, p-α-Syn, α-Syn, TH, p-Chk2, Chk2, p-C