ical source objects, image transformations, microscopy, anatomical structures, or model interface screenshots are visible; this is a quantitative performance comparison plot."]}, {"figure_index": 25, "page_no": 24, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 4 | Comparative evaluation of logical reasoning capabilities between XunZi-R and GPT-4o. a -x Radar plots showing reasoning performance across 24 MeSH disease categories, excluding neoplasms and nervous system diseases. Outer green bars indicate the recall of XunZi-R for each individual disease subtype within a category.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with 24 circular radar-style plots labeled **a–x**. Each panel corresponds to a medical or biological disease category:\n\na. Bacterial infections and mycoses  \nb. Psychological phenomena and processes  \nc. Digestive system diseases  \nd. Stomatognathic diseases  \ne. Mental disorders  \nf. Respiratory tract diseases  \ng. Otorhinolaryngologic diseases  \nh. Musculoskeletal diseases  \ni. Eye diseases  \nj. Urologic and male genital diseases  \nk. Cardiovascular diseases  \nl. Hemic and lymphatic diseases  \nm. Congenital abnormality  \nn. Skin diseases  \no. Metabolic diseases  \np. Endocrine system diseases  \nq. Immune system diseases  \nr. Disorders of environmental origin  \ns. Animal diseases  \nt. Pathology  \nu. Occupational diseases  \nv. Chemically induced disorders  \nw. Wounds and injuries  \nx. Behavior and behavior mechanisms\n\nEach panel contains an outer green circular histogram/ring with many radial ticks or bars and an inner radar/spider plot with overlaid colored polygon traces. The visible legend identifies two model/interface traces: **XunZi-R** in blue and **GPT-4o** in orange. The plots appear to compare model performance or scores across subcategories within each disease/biomedical class, using radial axes with visible numeric markings such as 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and 1.\n\nBiological/medical source objects represented are disease and disorder categories rather than microscopy objects or anatomical images. The figure visually reports comparative findings across biomedical taxonomy groups: XunZi-R and GPT-4o have similar radar profiles in many categories, with category-specific differences visible in the inner polygons. No exact quantitative values can be read reliably from the extracted image alone."]}, {"figure_index": 26, "page_no": 25, "caption": "Extended Data Fig. 5 | See next page for caption.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle visible panel labeled **a**. It is a bar chart showing **F1 score** on the y-axis, ranging from 0.0 to 1.0, comparing multiple language/model systems on the x-axis.\n\nVisible x-axis labels:\n- XunZi-R\n- Claude 3.7 Sonnet\n- Claude Sonnet 4\n- Claude Sonnet 4.5\n- DeepSeek-R1\n- DeepSeek-V3\n- GPT-4o\n- GPT-5\n- o3\n- GPT-4\n\nThe tallest bar is **XunZi-R**, around **0.90 F1 score**. Other models cluster around approximately **0.64-0.69**, with **GPT-4o** and **GPT-4** among the higher non-XunZi-R values.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, tissue samples, transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 27, "page_no": 27, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel stacked bar chart comparing counts of molecules across diseases and omics types.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Y-axis: “Num. of molecules”, ranging up to slightly above 50,000.\n- X-axis: disease categories, mostly cancers followed by neurodegenerative diseases.\n- Legend title: “Omics type”.\n- Color encoding:\n  - Blue: Transcriptomics\n  - Green: Proteomics\n  - Orange: Phosphoproteomics\n- Group labels beneath the x-axis:\n  - “Cancer”\n  - “Neurodegenerative diseases”\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Disease entities including melanoma, colon cancer, breast cancer, rectal cancer, glioblastoma, esophageal cancer, acute myeloid leukemia, lung adenocarcinoma, thyroid cancer, ovarian cancer, pancreatic cancer, liver cancer, bladder cancer, thymoma, sarcoma, stomach cancer, testicular cancer, kidney papillary cell carcinoma, lung squamous cell carcinoma, Alzheimer’s disease, Huntington’s disease, Lewy body dementia, Parkinson’s disease, and progressive supranuclear palsy.\n- Molecular measurement categories from omics datasets: transcriptomics, proteomics, and phosphoproteomics.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- No microscopy, imaging transformation, computational model architecture, or model interface is shown.\n- The data are transformed into stacked categorical bars, where total bar height represents the combined number of molecules across omics types for each disease.\n\nFindings visible from the chart:\n- Alzheimer’s disease has the highest total molecule count, exceeding 50,000, with large transcriptomics and proteomics contributions and a smaller phosphoproteomics segment.\n- Most cancer categories have total molecule counts roughly between 20,000 and 40,000.\n- Neurodegenerative diseases vary substantially, with Alzheimer’s disease highest and Parkinson’s disease lower than Alzheimer’s and progressive supranuclear palsy.\n- Transcriptomics and proteomics are present for all shown diseases; phosphoproteomics appears as an additional orange segment for many but not all categories."]}, {"figure_index": 28, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a bar chart labeled panel “a” with the title “Pan-cancer-AUPR”. The y-axis is labeled “AUPR” and ranges from 0.0 to about 0.8+. The x-axis compares six methods/models: XunZi, XunZi-R, XunZi-M, DNN, GPT-4o, and SVM.\n\nVisible values above bars:\n- XunZi: 0.799\n- XunZi-R: 0.736\n- XunZi-M: 0.716\n- DNN: 0.599\n- GPT-4o: 0.576\n- SVM: 0.550\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, or workflow transformations are visible in this extracted panel. The figure reports comparative model performance on a pan-cancer task using AUPR, with XunZi showing the highest AUPR among the displayed methods."]}, {"figure_index": 29, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel `d` shows a bar chart titled `Pan-cancer-AUPR`. The y-axis is labeled `AUPR`, ranging roughly from 0.0 to 0.8+, and the x-axis is labeled `CTD testing`.\n\nVisible model/method labels and values:\n- `XunZi-R`: 0.791\n- `XunZi`: 0.601\n- `XunZi-M`: 0.477\n- `GPT-4o`: 0.475\n- `DNN`: 0.416\n- `SVM`: 0.407\n\nThe figure compares predictive performance across methods on CTD testing using AUPR. No biological source objects, microscopy, anatomical structures, molecular diagrams, transformations, or model interface schematics are visible in this extracted panel. The main finding shown is that `XunZi-R` has the highest AUPR, followed by `XunZi`, while `SVM` is lowest among the displayed methods."]}, {"figure_index": 30, "page_no": 28, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle visible panel labeled **g**. It is a bar chart titled **“Neurodegenerative diseases-AUPR”**.\n\nVisible axes and labels:\n- Y-axis: **AUPR**, ranging approximately from 0.0 to 0.7.\n- X-axis categories: **Xunzi**, **Xunzi-M**, **Xunzi-R**, **GPT-4o**, **SVM**, **DNN**.\n\nDisplayed values:\n- Xunzi: **0.624**\n- Xunzi-M: **0.606**\n- Xunzi-R: **0.515**\n- GPT-4o: **0.424**\n- SVM: **0.326**\n- DNN: **0.303**\n\nBiological/source objects: The figure refers to **neurodegenerative diseases**, but no biological images, tissues, cells, genes, proteins, or datasets are visibly shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: The plot compares predictive/model methods by AUPR; visible methods include Xunzi variants, GPT-4o, SVM, and DNN. No architecture, workflow, or model interface is shown.\n\nFinding: **Xunzi has the highest AUPR**, followed by **Xunzi-M** and **Xunzi-R**; **DNN** is lowest among the shown methods."]}, {"figure_index": 31, "page_no": 28, "caption": "",